Theorem PL-5: no pass relies on stale summaries — every summary is read strictly after its latest production, and the Step 4a rollback-and-replay supersedes discarded TRMC-rewritten summaries

  Preconditions:
    - The AIMS pipeline is a 12-step strictly-ordered sequence per
      Annex E §AIMS + arc.md sec-Pipeline. PL-5 is the cross-cutting
      sequencing invariant over the WHOLE schedule (not a single ordered
      pair): every summary a pipeline step produces SHALL be observed by its
      downstream consumers at the values current at consumption time. A
      consumer reading a summary produced BEFORE the latest mutation of that
      summary reads a STALE value — a sequencing bug.
        Step 1: analyze_program() — produces MemoryContract map
        Step 3: compute_var_reprs() — produces ValueRepr (var_reprs)
        Step 3a: normalize_function() — TRMC rewrite (may mutate IR)
        Step 4: analyze_function() — produces converged AimsStateMap
        Step 4a: verify_trmc_soundness() — on TRMC failure rolls back to
                                            pre-TRMC IR + re-runs Steps 3-4,
                                            REPLACING ValueRepr + AimsStateMap
        Step 5: realize_rc_reuse() — consumes AimsStateMap (phase 1)
        Step 10: realize_annotations() — consumes AimsStateMap (phase 2)
    - PL-5 statement (per Annex E §AIMS PL-5 + arc.md
      sec-Non-Negotiable-Invariants #3): "No pass may rely on stale
      summaries. If a pipeline step modifies IR or updates an effect
      summary, all downstream consumers must see updated values. A verifier
      that runs before its inputs are available is a sequencing bug."
    - PL-5 type signature (per Pipeline.proof lines 48-52, the sorry
      obligation this theorem discharges):
        type : Pipeline -> Bool
        no pass SHALL rely on stale summaries; if a pipeline step modifies
        IR or updates an effect summary, all downstream consumers MUST see
        updated values (verifier-before-input = sequencing bug).
    - PL-5 is gated end-to-end by the Ori-owned checker; it is NOT in §15's
      Lean 4 critical-proof subset (scoped to §01A bootstrap + §08 RL-31 +
      §11 coexistence handshake only per section-07 success criterion line
      10). §15 lists §07 in depends_on solely to recurse
      aims-proof/proofs/07-pipeline/ into the CI checker corpus.
    - Shipped per-function driver: ori_arc::pipeline::aims_pipeline::
      run_aims_pipeline (compiler/ori_arc/src/pipeline/
      aims_pipeline/mod.rs lines 163-287). The summary production / consumption
      straight-line statements within one body:
        (a) Step 3 — `func.var_reprs = compute_var_reprs(...)` runs inside
            trmc::normalize_with_trmc (mod.rs lines 169-170 dispatch; the
            assignment is at trmc.rs lines 47-50). Produces ValueRepr.
        (b) Step 4 — `let state_map = { ... analyze_function(func, ...) }`
            (mod.rs lines 182-191). Produces the converged AimsStateMap; it
            reads config.contracts (Step 1's MemoryContract map, mod.rs line
            187) and &norm_result.context_regions (Step 3a output, mod.rs
            line 188).
        (c) Step 4a — `let (state_map, trmc_rewrite_survived) =
            verify_trmc_soundness(func, state_map, ...)` REBINDS `state_map`
            (mod.rs lines 195-196). The `let (state_map, ...)` shadows the
            Step-4 binding — every later read of `state_map` resolves to the
            REBOUND value, never the pre-Step-4a one.
        (d) Step 5 — `realize_rc_reuse(func, &state_map, ...)` (mod.rs lines
            246-256) reads the REBOUND `state_map`.
        (e) Step 10 — `realize_annotations(func, ..., state_map, ...)` via
            apply_phase_2_annotations (mod.rs lines 277 + 291-300) reads the
            REBOUND `state_map`.
    - Shipped Step 4a rollback-and-replay: ori_arc::pipeline::aims_pipeline::
      trmc::verify_trmc_soundness (compiler/ori_arc/src/
      pipeline/aims_pipeline/trmc.rs lines 109-162). On TRMC structural-
      verification failure (errors non-empty, trmc.rs lines 121-127) it
      restores the pre-TRMC function and RE-RUNS Steps 3-4 on the restored
      CFG:
        - `*func = original;` restores pre-TRMC IR (trmc.rs line 144).
        - `func.var_reprs = compute_var_reprs(func, ...)` RECOMPUTES ValueRepr
          for the restored IR (trmc.rs line 145) — superseding the discarded
          TRMC-rewritten var_reprs.
        - `func.var_rc_strategies = compute_var_rc_strategies(...)` refreshes
          the PIN-6 strategy cache (trmc.rs line 147).
        - `let restored_regions = normalize_function(func, None).context_regions`
          re-detects context regions on the restored CFG (trmc.rs lines 149-150).
        - `let restored_map = analyze_function(func, ..., &restored_regions,
          restored_immortals)` RECOMPUTES the AimsStateMap (trmc.rs lines
          151-157) — superseding the discarded TRMC-rewritten state map.
        - `(restored_map, false)` returns the RESTORED map as the function's
          result (trmc.rs line 158); run_aims_pipeline binds THIS to
          `state_map` (mod.rs line 195).
      The pre-TRMC function is saved BEFORE the rewrite for exactly this
      rollback (trmc.rs lines 56-64 save `pre_trmc_func`; the doc comment
      trmc.rs lines 13-14 states "`pre_trmc_func` is saved before TRMC
      rewrite for semantic rollback"). The rewrite is idempotent — "at most 2
      iterations" (trmc.rs line 12 + the debug_assert at trmc.rs lines 80-86).
    - This theorem reasons over the LINEAR per-function pipeline DAG's
      SUMMARY-DEPENDENCY graph: each summary (MemoryContract, ValueRepr,
      AimsStateMap) has a LATEST production position, and every consumer
      reads at a STRICTLY-LATER position than that latest production. PL-5 is
      downstream of PL-1/PL-1a (interprocedural prefix populated once) and
      PL-2/PL-3 (the ordered pairs that keep the AimsStateMap consumers after
      its producer); it is the umbrella sequencing invariant those ordered
      pairs each contribute to.

  Soundness property:
    Forall function F processed by run_aims_pipeline, and forall summary S in
    {MemoryContract, ValueRepr (var_reprs), AimsStateMap}.
      Let prod_latest(S) = position of the LATEST statement that produces or
      mutates S on F (the Step-4a re-run counts as a production when rollback
      fires), and let reads(S) = the set of positions at which a downstream
      pass reads S.
      Two conjuncts SHALL hold:

      (P1) Read-after-latest-production:
        Forall r in reads(S). prod_latest(S) < r.
        Every consumer reads S strictly after the last statement that
        produced or mutated S — no consumer observes a value superseded by a
        later mutation.

      (P2) Rollback-supersession (Step 4a replay):
        When Step 4a TRMC rollback fires on F, the ValueRepr recomputed at
        Step 4a (trmc.rs line 145) and the AimsStateMap recomputed at Step 4a
        (trmc.rs lines 151-157) are the LATEST productions of those summaries:
          prod_latest(ValueRepr) = position(Step 4a re-run)
          prod_latest(AimsStateMap) = position(Step 4a re-run)
        and BOTH are strictly less than every downstream consumer position
        (Step 5 realize_rc_reuse, Step 10 realize_annotations). The discarded
        TRMC-rewritten ValueRepr + AimsStateMap (produced at Steps 3/4 BEFORE
        the rollback) are NOT read by any downstream pass: the `let (state_map,
        ...)` rebind at mod.rs line 195 shadows the pre-rollback binding, and
        the restored func's var_reprs field (trmc.rs line 145) overwrites the
        rewritten field in place.

  Proof obligation:
    Two-part constructive discharge corresponding to (P1) and (P2), by
    structural induction over the per-function summary-dependency DAG +
    case analysis on the Step 4a rollback / no-rollback split.

    Part (P1) — Read-after-latest-production (structural induction over the
    summary-dependency DAG):

      Base case — MemoryContract (produced Step 1, consumed Step 4):
        Step 1 analyze_program produces the MemoryContract map ONCE over the
        whole program before the per-function loop (PL-1). Step 4
        analyze_function reads it via config.contracts (mod.rs line 187).
        No pipeline step mutates the contract map mid-per-function-loop, so
        prod_latest(MemoryContract) = position(Step 1) < position(Step 4).
        The contract read is non-stale.

      Inductive step — ValueRepr (produced Step 3, possibly re-produced
      Step 4a, consumed Step 4 + downstream):
        Step 3 compute_var_reprs assigns func.var_reprs (trmc.rs lines
        47-50). Step 4 analyze_function reads var_reprs through the
        ArcFunction it analyzes (mod.rs lines 184-190). If NO rollback fires,
        prod_latest(ValueRepr) = position(Step 3) < position(Step 4). If
        rollback fires, Step 4a re-produces var_reprs (trmc.rs line 145) AND
        re-runs Step 4 (trmc.rs lines 151-157) on the restored IR, so the
        Step-4a-produced var_reprs feed the Step-4a-rebuilt state map — still
        produced-before-consumed within the rollback block. By the induction
        hypothesis each downstream read of ValueRepr (via the AimsStateMap it
        fed) follows its latest production.

      Inductive step — AimsStateMap (produced Step 4, rebound Step 4a,
      consumed Step 5 + Step 10):
        Step 4 binds `state_map` (mod.rs line 182). Step 4a REBINDS it (mod.rs
        line 195) — the `let (state_map, ...)` shadow means every later
        identifier `state_map` resolves to the Step-4a value. Step 5
        realize_rc_reuse reads `&state_map` (mod.rs line 250) and Step 10
        realize_annotations reads `state_map` (mod.rs line 277 +
        apply_phase_2_annotations). Both reads are at positions strictly
        greater than position(Step 4a). Therefore prod_latest(AimsStateMap) =
        position(Step 4a) < min(position(Step 5), position(Step 10)). No
        consumer reads the pre-Step-4a state map.

      Termination:
        The three summaries {MemoryContract, ValueRepr, AimsStateMap} are the
        only cross-step summaries on the per-function suffix; each has been
        shown to be read strictly after its latest production. (P1) discharged.

    Part (P2) — Rollback-supersession (case analysis on the Step 4a split):

      Case no-rollback (did_trmc_transform false, OR TRMC verified clean):
        verify_trmc_soundness returns the INPUT state_map unchanged (trmc.rs
        lines 116-117 early-return when did_trmc_transform is false; trmc.rs
        lines 128-131 return the verified map when errors is empty). The
        Step-4-produced summaries are the latest productions; downstream reads
        (Step 5, Step 10) follow them. No staleness.

      Case rollback (did_trmc_transform true AND TRMC verification failed):
        errors is non-empty (trmc.rs lines 121-127). The rollback block
        (trmc.rs lines 143-158) executes:
          - restores pre-TRMC IR (`*func = original`, trmc.rs line 144) —
            the TRMC-rewritten IR is DISCARDED;
          - recomputes var_reprs for the restored IR (trmc.rs line 145) —
            the TRMC-rewritten ValueRepr is SUPERSEDED in the same field;
          - re-detects context regions (trmc.rs lines 149-150);
          - recomputes the AimsStateMap (`restored_map`, trmc.rs lines
            151-157) — the TRMC-rewritten state map is SUPERSEDED;
          - returns `(restored_map, false)` (trmc.rs line 158).
        run_aims_pipeline binds restored_map to `state_map` (mod.rs line 195),
        shadowing the discarded pre-rollback binding. Every downstream pass
        (Step 5 realize_rc_reuse, Step 10 realize_annotations) reads the
        restored_map / restored var_reprs — NOT the discarded TRMC-rewritten
        summaries. prod_latest(ValueRepr) = prod_latest(AimsStateMap) =
        position(Step 4a re-run), each < every downstream consumer position.
        No downstream pass observes the stale rewritten state. (P2) discharged.

    Coverage gate — summary-dependency edge enumeration:

      The verifier models the shipped per-function schedule as an ordered
      list of (summary, production-position, [read-positions]) records and
      discharges, for every summary S and every read r of S, the predicate
      prod_latest(S) < r. It also models the Step 4a rollback as a
      SECOND production of {ValueRepr, AimsStateMap} at the Step-4a position,
      asserts that production is the latest before any downstream read, and
      asserts the discarded pre-rollback productions have NO surviving read
      (the rebind shadows them).

      Coverage axes:
        Read-after-production (P1): the three summary records {MemoryContract,
          ValueRepr, AimsStateMap}, each verified read strictly after its
          latest production across all its consumers.
        Rollback-supersession (P2): the Step-4a re-production of {ValueRepr,
          AimsStateMap} verified as the latest production whose value every
          downstream consumer reads; the pre-rollback productions verified to
          have zero surviving downstream read.

    Negative-direction witness — read-before-production rejected:

      Hypothetically scheduling Step 5 (realize_rc_reuse) to read the
      AimsStateMap BEFORE Step 4 binds it (or before Step 4a rebinds it on
      rollback) would have realize_rc_reuse consume an absent or
      pre-convergence map — exactly the "verifier that runs before its inputs
      are available" sequencing bug PL-5 forbids (arc.md
      sec-Non-Negotiable-Invariants #3). The verifier rejects any schedule
      with a read of S at a position <= prod_latest(S).

    Negative-direction witness — stale-rewritten-summary read rejected:

      Hypothetically scheduling Step 5 to read the PRE-rollback (TRMC-
      rewritten) state map after the rollback discarded the rewritten IR
      would feed realization a summary computed against IR that no longer
      exists. The shipped rebind at mod.rs line 195 + the in-place var_reprs
      overwrite at trmc.rs line 145 make the rewritten summaries
      unreachable to downstream reads. The verifier rejects any schedule in
      which a downstream consumer reads a summary production that a later
      rollback re-production superseded.

    Cycle-soundness witness — PL-5 is the umbrella the ordered pairs serve:

      PL-5 is the cross-cutting sequencing invariant; the ordered-pair rules
      PL-1/PL-1a/PL-2/PL-3/PL-4/PL-4a each fix one boundary that contributes
      to it. Its antecedents are PL-1 (Step 1 contract map populated once),
      PL-2 (Step 4 before Step 5 — state map produced before consumed), and
      PL-10 (the TRMC rollback that re-runs Steps 3-4 on the restored CFG
      per PL-5). PL-5 produces no summary the ordered-pair rules consume; it
      observes their ordering. The dependency chain is acyclic:
        PL-1 + PL-1a --> PL-5 (prefix-once + callee-first feed the
                              non-stale contract / state-map productions)
        PL-2 --> PL-5 (Step 4 before Step 5 keeps the state map
                              non-stale at its consumer)
        PL-10 --> PL-5 (rollback re-runs Steps 3-4 so the restored
                              summaries supersede the rewritten ones)
      PL-5 is a sink of this dependency tree.

    Algorithm-citation declaration:
      property: pipeline summary-staleness freedom — every summary
                           (MemoryContract, ValueRepr, AimsStateMap) is read
                           strictly after its latest production, and the
                           Step 4a rollback re-production supersedes the
                           discarded TRMC-rewritten summaries
      published: standard "reaching-definitions / no-stale-read"
                           dataflow discipline — a use must observe the
                           latest reaching definition, never a definition a
                           later assignment killed; the same shape governs
                           SSA def-use validity and the analysis-summary
                           invalidation discipline in Lean 4 src/Lean/
                           Compiler/IR cited in arc.md sec-Reference Repos
      determinism: deterministic — the straight-line `let state_map`
                           (mod.rs line 182) then `let (state_map, ...)` rebind
                           (mod.rs line 195) then `&state_map` read (mod.rs
                           line 250) is a fixed shadowing order enforced by
                           Rust's lexical scoping; the in-place var_reprs
                           overwrite (trmc.rs line 145) is a fixed field
                           assignment; no schedule freedom exists
      reference impl: ori_arc::pipeline::aims_pipeline::run_aims_pipeline
                           (mod.rs Step 3 dispatch lines 169-170, Step 4
                           analyze_function lines 182-191, Step 4a
                           verify_trmc_soundness rebind lines 195-196, Step 5
                           realize_rc_reuse lines 246-256, Step 10 via
                           apply_phase_2_annotations lines 277 + 291-300) +
                           ori_arc::pipeline::aims_pipeline::trmc::
                           verify_trmc_soundness (trmc.rs lines 109-162:
                           rollback restore line 144, var_reprs recompute
                           line 145, AimsStateMap recompute lines 151-157,
                           restored-map return line 158) + the pre-TRMC save
                           (trmc.rs lines 56-64 + doc lines 13-14)

    Engines dispatched:
      structural_induction (PRIMARY — structural induction over the
        per-function summary-dependency DAG per section_07::
        verify_pl5_no_stale_summaries; the verifier models each summary's
        latest production position + its read positions and discharges (P1)
        read-after-latest-production via the predicate prod_latest(S) < r for
        every read r, and (P2) rollback-supersession via the Step-4a
        re-production being the latest production whose value every downstream
        consumer reads while the discarded pre-rollback productions have zero
        surviving read; emits Fail on any read-before-production or
        stale-rewritten-read violation)
      case_analysis (SECONDARY — per-case grid over the no-rollback vs
        rollback Step 4a split; gracious-accept once structural_induction has
        discharged the staleness obligation per the coverage-manifest PL row)

  Expected:
    status: valid
    reason: section_07 structural_induction engine discharges PL-5 via the per-function summary-dependency DAG — (P1) every summary (MemoryContract Step 1, ValueRepr Step 3, AimsStateMap Step 4) is read strictly after its latest production (prod_latest(S) < r for every read r) and (P2) the Step 4a rollback re-production supersedes the discarded TRMC-rewritten summaries (verify_trmc_soundness recomputes var_reprs at trmc.rs line 145 + AimsStateMap at trmc.rs lines 151-157 on the restored CFG, run_aims_pipeline rebinds state_map at mod.rs line 195 shadowing the pre-rollback binding, so Step 5 realize_rc_reuse and Step 10 realize_annotations read the restored non-stale map); grounded in the shipped run_aims_pipeline driver (mod.rs lines 163-287) + trmc::verify_trmc_soundness (trmc.rs lines 109-162); PL-5 is gated end-to-end by the Ori-owned checker, NOT in §15's Lean 4 critical-proof subset (§01A + §08 RL-31 + §11 only per section-07 success criterion line 10); case_analysis secondary-accepting per the coverage-manifest PL row
